WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.067
-Our first guest
is an Oscar-nominated actress

00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:05.400
who produces and stars
in the film

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.934
"The Eyes of Tammy Faye,"

00:00:06.934 --> 00:00:09.634
with is available right now
on HBO Max.

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:12.200
Please welcome Jessica Chastain!

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.601 --> 00:00:16.033
Yeah! Oh!

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:18.868
-Hi, honey.
-It is so great to see you.

00:00:18.868 --> 00:00:20.601
It is so great to see you.
-Good to see you.

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:23.100
-Thank you so much
for coming back on the show.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.434
This looks interesting.

00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:26.901
Can you tell everyone
where you're Zooming from?

00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:29.367
-I am Zooming
from the makeup trailer

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:32.000
from the George Jones
and Tammy Wynette

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.334
mini series that I'm shooting.
-Wow.

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.234
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:39.467
-How's that going?
-It's going, man.

00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:42.267
I mean, Michael Shannon
is playing George Jones.

00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:43.801
I know he loves you.

00:00:43.801 --> 00:00:45.367
-I love Michael Shannon!
-Yes.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.968
You guys
are ridiculous together.

00:00:47.968 --> 00:00:49.334
-He's the greatest.

00:00:49.334 --> 00:00:51.100
-And we're doing
a bunch of songs.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.100
You know, we're doing
like 30 songs together.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.834
And it's pretty intense.

00:00:54.834 --> 00:00:57.934
But I'm happy to take a break
to talk to you.

00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:00.200
-I'm so happy.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.033
I know -- Did you record with --

00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:05.033
You just recorded, like,
with a major producer, right?

00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:07.133
-A major producer.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:10.501
I was just in Nashville
with T Bone Burnett.

00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:11.734
And --
[ Applause ]

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:13.200
Yeah.
-Not too shabby.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.067
Not too shabby right there.

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:18.534
How did you like Nashville?

00:01:18.534 --> 00:01:20.934
-Well, I didn't really see
a lot of Nashville.

00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:23.601
I was mostly in the recording
studio, which, actually,

00:01:23.601 --> 00:01:25.601
it comes right up
against the cemetery

00:01:25.601 --> 00:01:30.300
that George Jones and Tammy
have been laid to rest at.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.033
So when we'd have breaks,
Mike and I

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:33.901
would walk through
and visit them.

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:36.601
I know, and then we drank
a lot of bourbon.

00:01:36.601 --> 00:01:38.834
A lot of bourbon
to get over the nerves.

00:01:38.834 --> 00:01:41.167
-Yeah.
-And sang with T Bone.

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.000
-That's amazing!
It's so -- I find --

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.200
I love that you're doing
more musical projects.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.300
Because I know where you live
right now is very famous for --

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.567
Some great music was made
in your building.

00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:55.334
-Yes, I live in the apartment
that Leonard Bernstein lived in

00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:57.434
when he wrote "West Side Story."

00:01:57.434 --> 00:01:58.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:00.801
It's kind of, like, insane.

00:02:00.801 --> 00:02:02.200
-Not too shabby.
-Not too shabby.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.934
-But, also, I think another cool
weird thing about where you live

00:02:05.934 --> 00:02:08.000
is that you spent a lot of time

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.567
around that building
when you were a kid,

00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:11.133
not even knowing
that you would eventually

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:12.567
live there, move there.

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:14.467
-Yeah, so,
when I went to college,

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:16.467
my grandmother
helped me get to New York,

00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:18.968
and, you know, she would
come and visit me and stuff.

00:02:18.968 --> 00:02:22.567
And on the weekends,
we would go to this diner

00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:25.801
and get baked ziti, and it was
such a big treat for me.

00:02:25.801 --> 00:02:27.767
And she would send me money
for it.

00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:29.834
And literally,
it's, like, across the street

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:32.300
from the restaurant
my grandma and I would go to.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.934
-Come on.
-It was really sweet.

00:02:33.934 --> 00:02:35.834
You're making me homesick,
Jimmy!

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:40.067
-[ Laughs ] We've got to go
next time when you come back.

00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:43.868
Speaking of growing up,
we have something

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:47.000
kind of in common
that I wanted to ask you about.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.033
At one point, we both got perms.

00:02:49.033 --> 00:02:52.100
-[ Laughter ]
-Yep.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.834
-I did it, yeah,
and immediately regretted it.

00:02:54.834 --> 00:02:57.434
But what was your --
what was your story?

00:02:57.434 --> 00:02:58.934
-I was --

00:02:58.934 --> 00:03:01.234
I mean, I was an awkward
redheaded kid,

00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:04.868
so my patron saint was Annie.

00:03:04.868 --> 00:03:07.534
And I wanted to look like her
so bad.

00:03:07.534 --> 00:03:10.701
So I cut my hair really short
and got a perm.

00:03:10.701 --> 00:03:12.167
And I remember,
after it happened --

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:13.667
It was, like, at the mall,
you know,

00:03:13.667 --> 00:03:15.200
and those chemicals, the smells.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:20.000
I looked at my mom,
and she was just horrified!

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.767
Like, "We made a huge mistake!"

00:03:22.767 --> 00:03:24.634
And, man,
I thought I looked great.

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:26.334
I lived for that perm.

00:03:26.334 --> 00:03:29.000
-Really?
How long did you rock the perm?

00:03:30.367 --> 00:03:33.200
-I -- I mean, I rocked it
until it grew out.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.434
I'm sure it's like -- My hair
was kind of, like, straight

00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:36.801
as it was growing out

00:03:36.801 --> 00:03:38.968
with, like, the poodle,
like, fluff at the bottom.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:42.601
-I went to a mall with
my friends and -- in college.

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:44.834
And, you know, you don't have
any money in college.

00:03:44.834 --> 00:03:47.467
So I went -- And so
a hair cut is a big deal.

00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:50.434
So, I got my hair cut,
and the girl cutting my hair,

00:03:50.434 --> 00:03:52.434
she was like,
"You have such straight hair.

00:03:52.434 --> 00:03:54.234
Such straight hair."
And I go, "Yeah.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.234
A lot of cow licks.
What are you gonna do?"

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:58.801
She was like,
"You should get a body wave."

00:03:58.801 --> 00:04:00.667
And I go, "What is that?
Like a perm?"

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:01.767
She's like, "No, no, no.

00:04:01.767 --> 00:04:03.200
It's not a perm.
It's a body wave.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.267
Gives you a little wave,
a little action to your hair."

00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:07.868
I go, "Well, there you go.

00:04:07.868 --> 00:04:10.367
You got to have goals
or wishes or dreams."

00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:13.200
She goes, "Actually,
I'm going to school.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.934
I could give you a body wave
for free."

00:04:15.934 --> 00:04:17.400
And all I heard was "free."

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.801
And when you're in college,
you're like, "Yeah, let's do it.

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:21.467
Let's do a body wave right now.
Let's do it."

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:24.300
So cut to -- I'm under
the dryer in the window

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.234
reading "Red Book,"
and my friends walk by.

00:04:26.234 --> 00:04:27.901
They're like,
"What are you doing?!"

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:29.834
I go, "I'm getting
a body wave, man."

00:04:29.834 --> 00:04:31.000
What are you talking about?"

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.234
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.234 --> 00:04:34.634
And they pulled
the hair dryer off.

00:04:34.634 --> 00:04:36.467
And I was like, "Oh, my gosh."

00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:41.601
Dude, it was -- it was crazy.
And you can't just wash it out.

00:04:41.601 --> 00:04:43.834
-Can I just ask you --
'cause this all sounds

00:04:43.834 --> 00:04:45.934
like a big ploy
to get a girl's number.

00:04:45.934 --> 00:04:49.267
[ Laughter ]

00:04:49.267 --> 00:04:51.567
Did you get her number
after she permed your hair?

00:04:51.567 --> 00:04:53.234
-No! I wasn't that -- What?!

00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:55.767
Who would ever want to date me
with this hair?!

00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:57.701
It was like
a Play-Doh helmet thing.

00:04:57.701 --> 00:04:58.834
It was so weird.

00:04:58.834 --> 00:05:01.200
It looked so weird on me.
It was odd.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.467
And I remember going home on
Christmas break to see my mom.

00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:06.734
And my mom opened the door,
and she slammed the door

00:05:06.734 --> 00:05:08.567
after she saw it.
-No.

00:05:08.567 --> 00:05:10.567
-Slammed the door and was like,
"What is wrong with you?

00:05:10.567 --> 00:05:12.334
What are you doing?"
I'm like, "I don't know."

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:14.234
-The face.
-I kind of looked like Kramer

00:05:14.234 --> 00:05:15.734
or something.
That's the way --

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:18.100
It was kind of --
-Isn't the expression

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.501
"the face and the hair
only a mother could love"?

00:05:20.501 --> 00:05:22.501
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, exactly.

00:05:22.501 --> 00:05:25.434
-You proved it wrong.
-Yeah. I proved it all wrong.

00:05:25.434 --> 00:05:28.200
You were phenomenal in
"The Eyes of Tammy Faye."

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.567
Congrats on all the nominations
you're getting.

00:05:31.567 --> 00:05:33.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:35.467
You didn't --

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:39.167
You don't look like you.
You don't sound like you.

00:05:39.167 --> 00:05:41.734
We had Andrew Garfield
on the show a couple months ago,

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:43.734
and he played Jim Bakker.

00:05:43.734 --> 00:05:45.667
And he told me
that you guys would go

00:05:45.667 --> 00:05:50.167
to church every Sunday
to kind of research this.

00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:52.300
The church that
they started, right?

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.033
-Yeah, so, every Sunday,

00:05:54.033 --> 00:05:56.133
pick up the Starbucks
and pick up Andrew,

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:57.567
and we'd drive to kind of --

00:05:57.567 --> 00:05:59.934
It's where Heritage USA was,

00:05:59.934 --> 00:06:02.801
which is now I think
MorningStar Church.

00:06:02.801 --> 00:06:05.434
And we were like, "Okay, we're
just gonna go and we're gonna,

00:06:05.434 --> 00:06:09.467
you know, be quiet and kind of
slip into the background,

00:06:09.467 --> 00:06:11.334
and hopefully
no one will notice us."

00:06:11.334 --> 00:06:14.734
We kinda felt like we were
sneaking in a little bit.

00:06:14.734 --> 00:06:17.734
We just wanted to have
the experience of being there.

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:21.167
And we're there, and right
in the beginning, Andrew goes,

00:06:21.167 --> 00:06:23.801
"I recognize that person
from the documentary!"

00:06:23.801 --> 00:06:25.100
And he was right.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.334
There was a man there
who was in the documentary.

00:06:27.334 --> 00:06:28.934
And I said,
"Okay. Let's go talk to him."

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:31.601
So we go up and we're super shy,
and I'm like, "Hello.

00:06:31.601 --> 00:06:34.234
My name is Jessica Chastain.
This is Andrew Garfield.

00:06:34.234 --> 00:06:37.133
And we're playing Tammy Faye
Bakker and Jim Bakker.

00:06:37.133 --> 00:06:38.934
And I'm wondering
if we can talk to you."

00:06:38.934 --> 00:06:40.501
And the guy
just like looks at us.

00:06:40.501 --> 00:06:42.634
And he's like, "Come with me."

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:44.801
And we're like,
"Oh, we're in trouble."

00:06:44.801 --> 00:06:48.667
So we start walking
behind the stage

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:51.267
and, like, these dark hallways.

00:06:51.267 --> 00:06:52.801
And we're thinking,
"We're gonna get busted.

00:06:52.801 --> 00:06:54.300
We're gonna
get thrown out of here."

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.934
I don't know what's happening."
And he takes us into this room

00:06:56.934 --> 00:06:59.400
where there's
other security guards.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.300
And they start whispering
to each other.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.200
And then all of a sudden,
he looks at us.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.100
I thought, "This is it.
We're in trouble."

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:08.834
He looks at Andrew and he goes,
"Are you Spider-Man?"

00:07:08.834 --> 00:07:10.434
[ Laughter ]

00:07:10.434 --> 00:07:12.033
And that's when I knew.
We're in!

00:07:12.033 --> 00:07:16.801
-Yes! "Am I Spid--
Yes, I happen to be Spider-Man."

00:07:16.801 --> 00:07:19.634
Absolutely.
-Yes!

00:07:19.634 --> 00:07:22.167
-I want to talk to you more
about "The Eyes of Tammy Faye."

00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:24.200
'Cause I want to know --
The singing voice is amazing.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.133
And the puppetry that you did.

00:07:26.133 --> 00:07:27.901
I want to get into
all of the stuff.

00:07:27.901 --> 00:07:30.968
More with Jessica Chastain
after the break. Stick around.

